the decisive  reason why hillary clinton was defeated  even though there are other factors that  need to be addressed but hillary clinton  never allowed a permanent inspector  general to be in the State Department  the four years she was there what if  there had been an inspector general who  very early on could have called  attention to the fact that the idea of  having your own email account and  putting the server in your home is  probably not the best idea in the world  the press which I think has suddenly got  its act together and certainly has been  very aggressive with its reporting in  the last 120 days did not do its job  during the campaign the false  equivalence the free ride that Donald  Trump got from even the Washington Post  in The New York Times was really an  embarrassment and I think most major  editors around the country understand  that now when I look at the court system  the federal courts have not been  aggressive in matters dealing with  national security where they give a lot  of benefit of the doubt to the  government and the state secrets  privilege or making people create their  own legal standing for bringing a case  to federal court so it's important that  citizens understand that these so-called  guardrails have to be fortified and that  there should be a certain amount of  standing of what a whistleblower is and  what a whistleblower does and going back  to warnings that we've gotten from  people such as James Madison and  Alexander Hamilton about the importance  of a free press the importance of not  trading off your Liberty for security  and the importance of a public servant  who exposes misconduct I think these are  things we have to keep in mind so we  have less than 10 minutes  [Applause]  the use of whistleblowers is unique to  this country because without our free  press wouldn't we be just no different  than the Soviet Union China or any other  dictatorship well I certainly don't  think it's unique to us and frankly  there are Soviet citizens and Russian  citizens who have taken far more risk  with their lives you know during the  worst of the hearings I tended to look  under the hood of my car before I  started my engine but I'd really didn't  think my life was threatened but in  places such as Russia and China  whistleblowers take tremendous risk in  Britain you had important whistleblowers  dealing with Tony Blair's dishonesty in  joining up with George Bush and the Iraq  war what is unique about the United  States I think and and I worry about  this uniqueness is the court system and  the investigative journalism when it  works people like Seymour Hersh Tony  Lewis when he was alive with the New  York Times James rise and with the New  York Times Greg Miller from the post  does a very good job but I notice now  and I don't know when it got there but  the new the masthead and the Washington  Post it was probably after Trump's  affection or inauguration democracy dies  in darkness go back and read Sinclair  Lewis's it can't happen here which was  written in the 1930s it's happening here  yeah  I think I heard you say earlier that the  primary responsibility of a civil  servant is to expose wrongdoing is that  correct I did is that Alexander Hamilton  by the way planted that seed at mine  okay well that probably answered my  question my question was should that be  the norm or is that an outcome of  current times in other words shouldn't  the primary responsibilities be to do  good as opposed to looking for  wrongdoing oh well I take doing good for  granted  okay I mean why would you do any tasks  by theirs with the government or outside  the government if you weren't having a  beneficial effect but when you get  beyond what are the standard obligations  of a public servant or anyone else  I think truthfulness and confronting  wrongdoing is important and remember it  was Alexander Hamilton and I always go  back to Hamilton first before I read any  of the other founding fathers because  there's so much wisdom there he talked  about impeachment in terms of the number  one offense that has to be addressed is  compromising the public trust and  certainly we've seen too much of that  whether it was Nixon whether it was  Reagan in iran-contra in the 1980s
 

